(project tuna-federation
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: federation - two tuna instances speak trees. Pre-registered as a sketch so the substrate never grows a feature that forecloses it. Thesis: the ops chain (genesis-anchored, hash-linked, prefix-folded, tamper-verified) is the replicable unit; peers exchange values by hash and converge by shipping verified ops windows, not by inventing a second protocol.")
 (* agent note (|2026-09-18, M12 opens with FED1 implemented: GET
    /api/fed/value/:hash answers {hash, kind, payload} for tree
    (canonical ternary; a program hash resolves as its own ternary -
    one address law) and bytes (base64).  Reads stay hash-gated
    (byte-values law 2), so any valid bearer identity may fetch and
    there is no new authz model; per-peer identities come from
    TUNA_FED_PEERS at boot (absent names get a generated token printed
    once, the root bootstrap pattern; names are lowercase alnum +
    dashes and gate ns/<peer>/ prefixes later).  Every answer, 404s
    included, lands in the ops chain as op "fed-value" attributed to
    the calling identity, so a peer's fetches are visible in the
    audited log.  Client side: scripts/fed-pull.sh rehashes the
    payload (openssl sha256) and fails loudly on mismatch or 404 -
    rejects are never absorbed.  Acceptance: tests/fed_tests.ml (6
    green, own scratch db) + dev-instance e2e (bytes fetch by a peer
    token, ops row carries the peer actor).|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-10-01, FED2 implemented: GET /api/fed/ops?
    prefix=&after_seq=&limit= returns a contiguous op window
    {ops[], head, after_seq, last_seq, limit, more, verified}; each op
    row carries its TRUE global predecessor hash (prev_hash, computed
    by SQL LAG over the whole log), so a prefix-filtered window - whose
    rows are non-contiguous - is still per-row verifiable
    (verify_window_rows recomputes sha256(prev_hash ^ row_concat)); a
    tampered row is a 500 with verified:false, never a silent pass.
    POST /api/fed/ops/apply folds a window into ns/<peer>/...: it
    re-verifies every row's chain, requires each effect's value to be
    held locally (pull values before ops, else 400 - rejects are
    journaled as op fed-apply, never absorbed), enforces per-peer
    authz (peer <name> writes only ns/<name>/...; admin any), and
    applies atomically under an optimistic head check (after_seq is the
    DESTINATION head; a moved head is 409).  The destination index is
    re-derived by fold: effect rows get tree_paths entries with a FRESH
    version (first-seen 1, re-apply version+1) - versions are local,
    the value hash is the shared fact - while EVERY applied source row
    appends one destination log row (put for effects, cas proof rows
    for reads/denials/fork markers), so after_seq advances contiguously.  
    Conflict law v0 holds: append-only, no merge; a re-apply of an
    existing path SHADOWS (fresh version reported under "shadowed"),
    divergence surfaces as two namespaces, never silently reconciled.
    scripts/fed-sync.sh drives a full value-pull + ops-pull + apply
    round between two instances.  Acceptance: tests/fed_tests.ml
    ops-chain-sync group (5 green: pull/apply roundtrip, shadow, five
    reject classes, admin-any-ns) + dev-instance e2e.  A supplied
    TUNA_FED_PEER_TOKEN_<NAME> is honored (the same shared bearer on
    every instance; absent = generated-and-printed-once), and the apply
    body carries values inline ({hash,kind,payload}), rehashed at the
    boundary, so a peer needs no separate value-put grant and a value
    slice is self-contained.|) *)
 (status in-progress)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
